NORMAL ECG WAVEFORM (fundamentals)
           R
          / \
     P   /   \     T     U
    / \ /     \   / \   /
   /   Q       \ /   \ /
  /             S     \
------------------------------------------------
   PR     QRS     ST     QT
 interval interval segment interval

P   = atrial depolarization (contraction)
PR  = AV conduction time (atria → ventricles)
QRS = ventricular depolarization (contraction)
ST  = early ventricular repolarization
T   = ventricular repolarization (relaxation)
U   = sometimes present (Purkinje / afterpotentials)

Paper speed 25 mm/s:
1 small square = 0.04 s
1 large square = 0.20 s
Heart rate (regular) ≈ 300 ÷ large squares between QRS

------------------------------------------------
MNEMONIC: R R P W Q S T

R  RATE
   How fast is the heart beating?
   Count large squares between QRS complexes.
   < 3 large squares  →  >100 bpm  = tachycardia
   > 6 large squares  →  < 50 bpm  = bradycardia
   Normal adult: 60–100 bpm

R  RHYTHM
   Regular  = equal R-R intervals (same # of small squares)
   Irregular = variable R-R intervals
   Sinus arrhythmia is common (often respiratory)

P  P WAVE
   Is there 1 P wave before every QRS?
   Yes (1:1)     → sinus rhythm (starts at SA node)
   No P wave     → AF or other atrial activation
   >1 P per QRS  → AV block / conduction delay
   P wave = atrial depolarization

W  WIDTH of QRS
   Is QRS wider than 3 small squares (>0.12 s)?
   Yes → slow / abnormal ventricular conduction
   Consider bundle branch block or ventricular rhythm
   Normal QRS < 0.12 s (narrow)

Q  Q WAVE
   Are there deep / pathologic Q waves?
   Deep Q (>0.04 s and/or >25% of R wave)
   Usually old (chronic) myocardial infarction (scar)
   Q = first negative deflection of QRS
   Helps identify prior MI, not acute ischemia

S  ST SEGMENT
   Depression or elevation?
   Elevation in ≥2 contiguous leads → consider STEMI
   Depression / T inversion → consider NSTEMI / ischemia
   New LBBB can also indicate STEMI equivalent

T  T WAVE
   Abnormal inverted (upside-down) T waves?
   Inverted T can indicate ischemia or strain
   T = ventricular repolarization

------------------------------------------------
KEY INTERVALS (typical values)

P wave     ≤ 0.12 s   (≤3 small squares)
PR interval 0.12–0.20 s (3–5 small squares)
QRS        < 0.12 s   (<3 small squares)
QT         ~0.36–0.44 s (rate-dependent; ~9–11 small squares)

------------------------------------------------
STEMI vs NSTEMI (quick contrast)

STEMI
  ST elevation:
    limb leads ≥1 mm in ≥2 contiguous leads
    precordial ≥2 mm in ≥2 contiguous leads
  Pathophys: complete coronary occlusion
  Damage: transmural (full thickness)
  Troponin: elevated

NSTEMI
  ST depression (≥0.5 mm limb / ≥1 mm precordial)
  and/or T-wave inversion
  Pathophys: partial / transient occlusion
  Damage: subendocardial (partial thickness)
  Troponin: elevated

------------------------------------------------
LEADS & TERRITORIES (variables to localize)

LATERAL   I, aVL, V5, V6     → LCx (or diagonal)
INFERIOR  II, III, aVF       → RCA (or LCx)
ANTERIOR  V3, V4             → LAD
SEPTAL    V1, V2             → LAD (septal perforators)

LAD → septal (V1–V2) + anterior (V3–V4)
LCx → lateral (I, aVL, V5, V6)
RCA → inferior (II, III, aVF)

------------------------------------------------
WORKFLOW (use in order)
1. Rate
2. Rhythm
3. P waves present + 1:1 with QRS?
4. QRS width
5. Pathologic Q waves?
6. ST segment (elev / dep)
7. T waves (inversion?)
Then localize by leads if ischemic changes exist.

